+### `outcome` values
+
+`outcome` describes the worst real-world state a successful invocation can leave,
+judged by **who can put it back**. The three values are defined by a test rather
+than by adjectives, so that two independent classifiers reach the same value:
+
+| Value | Test | Examples |
+| :--- | :--- | :--- |
+| `benign` | Nothing changes outside the agent's working context. | Read a file, run a `SELECT`, search. |
+| `consequential` | State changes, but the principal can return to the prior state, or to an equivalent one at a known and bounded cost, through interfaces it already holds and without anyone else's consent. | Edit a file whose prior version is committed; save a draft; refund a mistaken charge. |
+| `irreversible` | Recovery needs a party outside the principal or out-of-band effort, or is impossible. | Send a message; publish; overwrite data that has no history; rotate a credential others depend on. |
+
+A tool that delivers content to people or systems outside the principal (a
+*disclosure*) MUST declare `irreversible`, even if the server can later delete the
+content: a recipient who has seen it cannot be made not to have seen it.
+
+`outcome` is a property of the tool, not of one call. A server MUST declare the
+value its most consequential permitted invocation can reach (a SQL tool that
+accepts `DROP TABLE` is `irreversible`, even though most of its calls are
+`benign`). A host MAY treat a specific call as less consequential only through a
+per-call mechanism such as Tool Resolution
+([SEP-1862](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/pull/1862)),
+never by lowering the declared value. A host SHOULD treat a missing `outcome` as
+`irreversible`, consistent with the default of `destructiveHint`.
+
+### Finer classification schemes (`outcomeRef`)
+
+`outcome` is a floor every client can act on. A deployment that needs finer
+distinctions (for example, between an action the principal can restore exactly
+and one it can only compensate, which call for different approval policies) MAY
+carry an `outcomeRef` alongside it:
+
+```jsonc
+"outcome": "consequential",
+"outcomeRef": {
+  "type": "reversibility-classes.v0.2",   // open string naming a scheme
+  "class": "R2",                          // a value in that scheme
+  "ref": "https://github.com/TwoMuchLabs/reversibility-classes/blob/main/registry/stripe.yaml"
+}
+```
+
+- `type` is an open string, coordinated like `evidenceRef.type`, so no single
+  classification model is baked into the wire.
+- A scheme MUST map each of its classes to exactly one `outcome` value. A producer
+  MUST NOT emit an `outcomeRef` whose class maps to a less consequential `outcome`
+  than the one declared.
+- A client that does not implement the scheme ignores `outcomeRef` and acts on
+  `outcome`.
+
+This mirrors how data-labelling schemes refine the `sensitive` boolean in
+[`trust-annotations`](./trust-annotations.mdx): a coarse wire value that cannot
+rot, refined behind an open `type` for hosts that implement the scheme.
+
+**Example scheme: Reversibility Classes.** [Reversibility Classes](https://github.com/TwoMuchLabs/reversibility-classes)
+(Apache-2.0) defines five classes by the same test, and maps them as follows:
+
+| Class | Name | `outcome` |
+| :--- | :--- | :--- |
+| R0 | No effect | `benign` |
+| R1 | Restorable: prior state recorded, principal can write it back, nobody outside saw it | `consequential` |
+| R2 | Compensable: a known fix exists at bounded cost | `consequential` |
+| R3 | Hard to recover: needs a human or out-of-band recovery | `irreversible` |
+| R4 | Irreversible, including every disclosure | `irreversible` |
+
+Its open registry classifies 536 tools across 20 MCP servers, each with a
+one-sentence rationale and a link to the source the tool names were checked
+against, and ships language-neutral conformance vectors. Because a third party
+assigns the classes, they are usable when the server is not trusted, which is the
+accountability model described in [`trust-model.md`](../../docs/trust-model.md).
+
